        "lines": "14-19",
        "snippet": "function withdraw(uint256 amount) external {\n    require(tx.origin == owner, \"not owner\");\n    require(balances[msg.sender] >= amount, \"insufficient\");\n    (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}(\"\");\n    require(ok, \"transfer failed\");\n    balances[msg.sender] -= amount;\n}"
      },
      "exploitScenario": "An attacker deploys a contract that calls `VulnerableVault.withdraw` in its receive/fallback function. On the first call the balance check passes, ETH is sent to the attacker contract, whose fallback immediately calls `withdraw` again before the original balance is decremented. This recurses until the vault is empty.",
      "recommendation": "Use the checks-effects-interactions pattern: decrement `balances[msg.sender]` before the external transfer. Additionally, consider using a reentrancy guard (e.g., OpenZeppelin's `ReentrancyGuard`) for all functions performing external calls.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "findingId": "tx-origin-owner-authorization",
      "title": "Dangerous tx.origin authorization in withdraw",
      "severity": "high",
      "description": "The `withdraw` function authorizes the caller using `tx.origin == owner`. This is vulnerable to phishing: if the owner interacts with any malicious contract, that contract can call `withdraw` on the owner's behalf and the `tx.origin` check will still succeed.",
      "evidence": {
        "file": "VulnerableVault.sol",
        "lines": "14-15",
        "snippet": "function withdraw(uint256 amount) external {\n    require(tx.origin == owner, \"not owner\");\n    require(balances[msg.sender] >= amount, \"insufficient\");"
      },
      "exploitScenario": "The owner visits a phishing dApp that calls `VulnerableVault.withdraw`. Because `tx.origin` is the owner's EOA, the require passes. The dApp can drain the owner's full recorded balance or, combined with reentrancy, the entire contract.",
      "recommendation": "Replace `tx.origin` checks with `msg.sender` checks, or better yet use role-based access control (OpenZeppelin `AccessControl`) and verify the caller's balance/permissions directly.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "findingId": "arbitrary-delegatecall-in-execute",
      "title": "Arbitrary delegatecall in execute can corrupt storage",
      "severity": "high",
      "description": "The `execute` function performs a `delegatecall` to any user-supplied `target` address. Because `delegatecall` executes the target's code in the context of `VulnerableVault`, a malicious or buggy target can overwrite critical storage slots such as `owner` or `balances`, leading to complete contract compromise.",
      "evidence": {
        "file": "VulnerableVault.sol",
        "lines": "23-27",
        "snippet": "function execute(address target, bytes calldata data) external {\n    require(msg.sender == owner, \"not owner\");\n    (bool ok, ) = target.delegatecall(data);\n    require(ok, \"delegatecall failed\");\n}"
